42 rules for Makefiles: no wildcards

Minimal impact. The entire Makefile architecture operates on SRC_FILES
as a variable — everything downstream
(the derived REL_OBJS, DBG_OBJS, LKS_OBJS, DEP_FILES, the pattern rules,
-MMD, the precondition guard) is agnostic to how that variable was populated.


```
rwildcard = $(foreach d,$(wildcard $1*),\
              $(call rwildcard,$d/,$2)\
              $(filter $(subst *,%,$2),$d))

SRC_FILES := $(call rwildcard,$(SRC_DIR)/,*.cpp)
```

becomes:
```
SRC_FILES := src/base/Foo.cpp \
             src/classes/Bar.cpp \
             ...
```


The rwildcard function definition is deleted entirely.
Nothing else changes — not the object derivation, not the pattern rules,
not the variant architecture, not the dependency system.

The precondition guard ifeq ($(SRC_FILES),) still works and is arguably more useful:
if you accidentally omit a file from the list, the guard won't fire
(it fires only on empty), but the linker will, which surfaces the omission immediately.

One thing to be aware of: the guard becomes weaker.
With rwildcard, an empty result means the src/ tree genuinely has no .cpp files —
a real misconfiguration. With an explicit list, SRC_FILES is never accidentally empty
unless the developer deletes every entry.
The guard's value shifts from "misconfiguration detector"
to "documentation of the contract". Not a problem, just a different meaning.

Pre-submission, this is a mechanical find-and-replace:
run
```
find src -name "*.cpp" | sort
```
format it as the assignment, delete rwildcard
